---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import PrivacyNotice from '@/components/PrivacyNotice.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '@/config/site';
import { buildSimplePageSchema } from '@/lib/schema';

const description = `Learn how ${siteConfig.name}, its contact provider, future consent platform, analytics providers, and Google AdSense may use cookies, browser storage, and identifiers.`;
const jsonLd = buildSimplePageSchema({
  type: 'WebPage',
  name: 'Cookie Policy',
  path: '/cookie-policy/',
  description,
  includePublisher: false,
});
---

<BaseLayout title="Cookie Policy" description={description} canonicalPath="/cookie-policy/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Cookie Policy' }]} />
  <article class="section-sm article-container prose">
    <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated: {siteConfig.updatedDate}</p>
    <PrivacyNotice>
      <strong>Launch configuration:</strong> Advertising, analytics, and the site-wide consent platform
      are disabled. The core device tests do not require cookies or persistent result storage. The
      external FormSubmit contact flow may use security or anti-spam technology when a message is sent.
    </PrivacyNotice>

    <p>
      This policy explains cookies and similar browser storage that may be used when visiting
      {siteConfig.name}. It will be updated if advertising, analytics, or a consent platform is enabled.
    </p>

    <h2>1. What cookies and similar storage are</h2>
    <p>
      Cookies are small values stored by a browser and sent with relevant requests. Similar
      technologies include local storage, session storage, IndexedDB, caches, and provider identifiers.
      Browser permission decisions are separate from cookies, although a browser may remember them.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Core device tests</h2>
    <p>
      The core tests use in-memory JavaScript state. They do not require an account, server session,
      database, cloud history, or persistent result storage. Reloading or closing the page clears the
      current test results.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Hosting and security</h2>
    <p>
      A static host, content-delivery network, or security provider may process requests and may use
      essential technical storage when necessary to deliver and protect the website. Actual behavior
      depends on the hosting configuration.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Contact form and spam protection</h2>
    <p>
      The Contact page sends submissions to FormSubmit. FormSubmit may use cookies, identifiers, or
      anti-spam services, including a human-verification step, when a form is submitted. These services
      are separate from the browser-local device tests and apply only to the contact workflow.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Consent preferences</h2>
    <p>
      A site-wide consent platform is disabled at launch because advertising and analytics are also
      disabled. If either is enabled later, a suitable consent platform may store the visitor's choices.
      A visible Privacy Choices control will then reopen that interface.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Advertising storage</h2>
    <p>
      Google AdSense and advertising vendors may use cookies or similar identifiers depending on
      region, consent, ad mode, browser settings, and provider policy. Advertising is disabled at launch.
      It will not be enabled without updated disclosures, required consent configuration, and a real
      AdSense publisher ID.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Analytics storage</h2>
    <p>
      Analytics is disabled at launch. If enabled later, the provider, purposes, storage names,
      retention, and consent requirements will be documented. Media, key contents, pointer or touch
      coordinates, contact content, and detailed diagnostic summaries will not be sent to analytics.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Current configuration</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Launch state</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Core device tests</td><td>No persistent storage required</td><td>Temporary browser-local diagnostics</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Contact form</td><td>Enabled through FormSubmit</td><td>Message delivery and spam prevention</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Advertising</td><td>Disabled</td><td>Not active at launch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Analytics</td><td>Disabled</td><td>Not active at launch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consent platform</td><td>Disabled</td><td>Enable only when needed for configured services</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>9. Managing browser storage</h2>
    <p>
      You can use browser settings to inspect, block, or delete cookies and storage. Blocking
      nonessential storage must not prevent the device-testing tools from working. Clearing site data
      may also reset saved permissions or provider choices.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
